<% @title = "Amber releases — dated framework and CLI notes" %>
<% @meta = "Current Amber Framework and Amber CLI releases, dates, beta boundaries, and notes synchronized from GitHub." %>

<header class="page-hero release-page-hero">
  <div class="site-container page-hero-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Release notes · synchronized from GitHub</p>
      <h1>Know what shipped.<br><em>And what beta means.</em></h1>
      <p>GitHub Releases remains the source of truth. This page turns those records into a dated, readable map of the stable framework, V2 web beta, and standalone CLI.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="release-sync-card">
      <span>Last synchronized</span>
      <strong><%= snapshot.synced_at == "Unavailable" ? snapshot.synced_at : Time.parse_rfc3339(snapshot.synced_at).to_s("%B %d, %Y at %H:%M UTC").gsub(" 0", " ") %></strong>
      <small>Automatic check every six hours</small>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
  <section class="section release-current-section">
    <div class="site-container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Current choices</p><h2>Three versions.<br>Three different jobs.</h2></div>
        <p>The framework runtime and the CLI now have independent releases. Pick the framework line first; use the current CLI when creating a new V2 application.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="release-choice-grid">
        <% if release = stable_release %>
          <article class="release-choice-card release-choice-stable">
            <span class="release-status">Stable framework</span>
            <h3><%= release.tag_name %></h3>
            <time datetime="<%= release.published_at %>"><%= release.published_on %></time>
            <p>The conservative Amber 1.x line for existing applications, with current Crystal compatibility and maintenance corrections.</p>
            <a href="<%= release.url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the official GitHub release →</a>
          </article>
        <% end %>
        <% if release = beta_release %>
          <article class="release-choice-card release-choice-beta">
            <span class="release-status">V2 web beta</span>
            <h3><%= release.tag_name %></h3>
            <time datetime="<%= release.published_at %>"><%= release.published_on %></time>
            <p>The current V2 framework tag and release-gated ECR web path. It is a prerelease, but it is not a promise of a difficult rewrite before stable.</p>
            <a href="/docs/v2/getting-started/installation">Test the supported web path →</a>
          </article>
        <% end %>
        <% if release = cli_release %>
          <article class="release-choice-card release-choice-cli">
            <span class="release-status">Standalone CLI</span>
            <h3><%= release.tag_name %></h3>
            <time datetime="<%= release.published_at %>"><%= release.published_on %></time>
            <p>The current application generator and development command. Its release cycle is independent from the framework dependency in your app.</p>
            <a href="<%= release.url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the official GitHub release →</a>
          </article>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section beta-explainer-section">
    <div class="site-container beta-explainer-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The word “beta,” unpacked</p>
        <h2>The web path is coherent.<br><em>The frontier stays named.</em></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="beta-explainer-copy">
        <p><strong>V2 is tagged as a prerelease</strong> while real applications test it in public. Routes, controllers, ECR views, configuration, schemas, WebSockets, jobs, and the clean web template form the web-framework core being release-gated.</p>
        <p><strong>Preview does not spread by association.</strong> Persistence, attachments, native applications, and the separate Asset Pipeline keep their own status. Their unfinished edges do not make an ordinary ECR application provisional.</p>
        <p><strong>V1 applications are not expected to be rewritten.</strong> Many ECR applications can begin—and some can finish—with the Amber version change in <code>shard.yml</code>. The migration guide identifies the bounded exceptions.</p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/v2/migration-guide">See the smallest safe upgrade path</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section release-history-section">
    <div class="site-container">
      <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Synchronized history</p><h2>Notes from the repositories.</h2></div>
        <p>Every entry links to its authoritative GitHub record. Expand an item here for the published release body.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="release-streams">
        <% [{"Amber Framework", framework_releases}, {"Amber CLI", cli_releases}].each do |stream| %>
          <section class="release-stream">
            <h3><%= stream[0] %></h3>
            <% stream[1].each do |release| %>
              <details class="release-note">
                <summary>
                  <span><strong><%= release.tag_name %></strong><small><%= release.name %></small></span>
                  <time datetime="<%= release.published_at %>"><%= release.published_on %></time>
                </summary>
                <div class="release-note-body">
                  <% unless release.body.empty? %><%= MarkdownPreprocessor.render(release.body, preprocess: false, safe: true) %><% end %>
                  <a href="<%= release.url %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the source release on GitHub →</a>
                </div>
              </details>
            <% end %>
          </section>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
